# [What FUSE is]($heading.id('what'))

FUSE (Filesystem in Userspace) lets an ordinary program implement a
filesystem. The kernel's FUSE module turns every operation on the mounted
folder (look up a name, list a folder, read some bytes at an offset) into a
request message. A user-space process reads that message from the device file
`/dev/fuse`, does the work, and writes back a reply. The program that issued
the call sees an ordinary filesystem. sshfs, rclone mount and many others
work this way.

Most FUSE filesystems use **libfuse**, a C library that hides the protocol
behind callbacks. NomadFS doesn't. Its driver (`src/fs/fuse.zig`) reads and
writes the kernel's binary protocol directly: fixed C-layout structs from
`linux/fuse.h`, protocol version 7.31. It needs neither libfuse nor libc,
and the whole driver is one Zig file you can read.

# [Mounting without root]($heading.id('mounting'))

Mounting normally takes root. FUSE's standard way around this is the setuid
helper `fusermount3`, from the `fuse3` package:

1. NomadFS creates a Unix socket pair and runs `fusermount3` with one end in
   the environment variable `_FUSE_COMMFD`.
2. `fusermount3` opens `/dev/fuse`, mounts it on your folder (options
   `nosuid,nodev,default_permissions`, `fsname=nomadfs`), and sends the open
   file descriptor back over the socket (`SCM_RIGHTS`).
3. NomadFS receives the descriptor and starts serving requests on it.

To unmount, NomadFS runs `fusermount3 -u -z` (a lazy unmount: the kernel
detaches the folder once nothing is using it). It does this on Ctrl-C, on
`SIGTERM`, or when you switch the mount off in the app.

# [The read path]($heading.id('read'))

[A read on the mount goes through the kernel to NomadFS, which loads only the blocks that cover it.](<$image.siteAsset('learn/fuse-read.svg').alt('A program runs cat on a file under ~/NomadFS/Laptop/Photos. Its read call goes to the Linux kernel, VFS then the fuse module, which sends a request over /dev/fuse to NomadFS. NomadFS runs one loop: read a request, handle it, write the reply, for operations such as LOOKUP, GETATTR, READDIR and READ. tree.readAt gets the blocks from the local store of cached blocks, or with GET_BLOCK to the device. Mounting happens once: fusermount3 opens /dev/fuse and mounts it, then hands the file descriptor back over a socketpair (_FUSE_COMMFD); no libfuse.')>)

The mount shows `<mount>/<device>/<share>/…` for every reachable device, and
`<mount>/Swarm`. NomadFS runs one loop: read a request from `/dev/fuse`,
handle it, write the reply.

- **LOOKUP / GETATTR** turn a name into an inode and its attributes. Each
  path keeps its inode number for the life of the mount. Device folders come
  from the devices NomadFS can reach, and share folders from their
  `GET_SHARES` listing.
- **READDIR** on a share folder is one directory block from the
  [Merkle DAG]($link.page('learn/merkle-dag').ref('folders')), fetched with
  `GET_BLOCK` the first time and from the local store after that.
- **READ** calls `tree.readAt` for the requested range. Only the blocks that
  cover those bytes are loaded, [each checked against its hash]($link.page('learn/sha-256').ref('verify')).
  `cat`, a video player that seeks, or `cp` all work the same way.

Shares are read-only: files show as `0444`, folders as `0555`, and writes
fail with `EROFS`. The kernel is told to cache attributes and names for one
second, so changes on other devices show up quickly.

# [The write path: the swarm folder]($heading.id('write'))

`<mount>/Swarm` is writable. But a swarm file is a tree of hashed blocks,
and you can't change one byte of it in place. NomadFS stages writes in an
ordinary file and turns the result into blocks when the file is closed.

[Writes go to a staging file; closing the file turns it into one swarm edit.](<$image.siteAsset('learn/fuse-write.svg').alt('Four steps. 1, open or create: NomadFS makes a staging file, copying the old content in. 2, write: bytes go to the staging file, and reads see them at once. 3, release, the last close: FastCDC and the DAG builder put the file into the block store, and one manifest edit is made with the clock plus one. 4, peers pull the manifest with GET_SWARM and fetch blocks when the file is opened.')>)

1. **OPEN for writing, or CREATE.** NomadFS makes a staging file next to the
   manifest (`<storage_path>.swarm.json.staging/`). Opening an existing file
   without `O_TRUNC` first copies its current content in, fetching blocks
   from other devices if needed, so appends and in-place edits work.
2. **WRITE** goes straight into the staging file with `pwrite`. While the file
   is open, **READ** is served from the staging file too, so a program sees
   what it just wrote.
3. **RELEASE** (the last close) commits. The staging file is chunked with
   [FastCDC]($link.page('learn/fastcdc')), its blocks go into the local store,
   and the manifest gets **one** new version of the path. Other devices
   never see half a write, however many `write` calls it took.
4. Within about 10 seconds the other devices pull the new manifest
   ([`GET_SWARM`]($link.page('learn/swarm-crdt').ref('sync'))) and see the
   new version. They fetch its blocks only when someone opens it.

The other operations become manifest edits directly: `mkdir` adds a folder
entry; `unlink` and `rmdir` write tombstones (`rmdir` is refused while the
folder has anything in it); `rename` is a move. That covers the editor trick
of writing a temporary file and renaming it over the original. A truncate
without an open file is staged and committed at once. Moving a file between
the swarm folder and a device's share fails with `EXDEV`, as a move between
two filesystems does.

# [Limits]($heading.id('limits'))

- Linux only. The mount uses Linux's `/dev/fuse` and `fusermount3`; other
  platforms use the app.
- Requests are handled one at a time, in order. A read that has to fetch a
  block from a slow device holds up the requests behind it.
- Extended attributes are not supported (`ENOSYS`).

Source: `src/fs/fuse.zig`, `src/fs/tree.zig`, `src/fs/swarm.zig`.
